; What `$` means inside a declaration header is what it means anywhere: the
; reader nests the rest of the list and hands the result on, because it does not
; know what a list is for. `$` before a `lambda`'s body is useful and `$` inside
; its parameter list is nonsense, and the nonsense lands on the shape error
; `ast.rs` already has for that list rather than on a second grammar in the
; reader (`D-150`).
(fn main () -> i32
  (let twice (lambda () ((value $ i64)) -> i64 value))
  0)
